aloha-oe -- sources and references

These are sources consulted in writing the score and synthesizer,
and in checking the system audio interfaces.  The program does not
fetch them at build time or run time.

Music
-----

Queen Liliuokalani, Aloha 'Oe.  J. Bodewalt Lampe arrangement,
Jerome H. Remick & Co., 1912, score pages 2-3.  The upper vocal line
is the principal source for src/melody.h.  IMSLP 786918.
https://s9.imslp.org/files/imglnks/usimg/3/38/IMSLP786918-PMLP58215-Liliuokalani-Aloha_Oe.pdf

Aloha 'Oe, W. A. Quincke & Co., 1912, score pages 2-3.
Used to check the Lampe transcription.  IMSLP 26121.
https://s9.imslp.org/files/imglnks/usimg/9/9f/IMSLP26121-PMLP58215-Liliuokalani_AlohaOe.pdf

OpenScore Lieder, lc6650166.mscx, transcribed from IMSLP 26121.
The file identifies its copyright status as OpenScore (CC0).
https://github.com/OpenScore/Lieder/blob/main/scores/Liliuokalani%2C_Queen_of_the_Hawaiian_Islands/_/Aloha_Oe/lc6650166.mscx
OpenScore's account of its transcription and checking procedure:
https://fourscoreandmore.org/scores-of-scores/notes-for-users/

Johnny Cash, Aloha Oe, American VI: Ain't No Grave.
Reference for the key, tempo and arrangement.
Official track page and Apple Music preview:
https://www.johnnycash.com/track/aloha-oe-2/
https://music.apple.com/us/song/1440802266

David R. Ferguson, interview on recording and mixing American VI.
Source for the account of the guitars and vocal recording.
https://www.soundonsound.com/techniques/secrets-mix-engineers-david-r-ferguson

Instrument synthesis
--------------------

Julius O. Smith, Physical Audio Signal Processing,
"Digital Sinusoid Generators".  Oscillators as two-dimensional
rotations, used for the harmonic renderer.
https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Digital_Sinusoid_Generators.html

David Politzer, paper on plucked strings.  Background on decomposing
the string motion into damped modes.
https://www.its.caltech.edu/~politzer/plucked-string-AJP.pdf

Extensions of the Karplus-Strong Plucked-String Algorithm.
Background for the alternative delay-loop method and its tuning.
The program uses direct harmonic synthesis instead.
https://musicweb.ucsd.edu/~trsmyth/papers/KSExtensions.pdf

Speech synthesis
----------------

Dennis H. Klatt, Journal of the Acoustical Society of America 67
(1980), 971-995.  DOI: 10.1121/1.383940.  Background on formant
speech synthesis.
https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/david/ma_ssp/doc/Klatt-1980-JAS000971.pdf

Csound reference manual, "Formant Values".  Reference values for
vowel formant frequencies and bandwidths.  No Csound code or library
is used.
https://csound.com/docs/manual/MiscFormants.html

Daniel Jurafsky and James H. Martin, Speech and Language Processing,
"Acoustic Phonetics and Signals".  Source/filter background.
https://web.stanford.edu/~jurafsky/slp3/old_aug24/H.pdf

KTH speech research papers.  Background on glottal sources,
aspiration and formant synthesis.
https://www.speech.kth.se/~rolf/papers/wwnasqpsr.pdf
https://www.speech.kth.se/qpsr/1991/1991_32_2-3_001-010.pdf

SAM, SAM2600, Tiny Speech Synth and Pink Trombone.  Examples of
compact speech synthesis, phoneme scheduling and source/filter
models.  The IPA planner and its parameters were written separately;
no SAM code, phoneme recordings or SAM parameter tables are included.
https://github.com/s-macke/SAM
https://rossumur.github.io/SAM2600/
https://heckmeck.de/demoscene/tiny-speech-synth-js/
https://github.com/IMAGINARY/pink-trombone

NESdev, APU DMC.  Background on the sample-playback hardware often
associated with early game speech.  The retro voice is not a DMC
emulation and contains no sampled speech.
https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/APU_DMC

